    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks for these. They're a good start, but they feel a bit generic—lots of 'tell me about a time' questions that people have canned answers for. \n\nI want to make sure we're really hitting our core values. Could you try a slightly different version of these? I need them to be more pointed. Specifically, I'd like to see if you can weave in those specific themes we're looking for (innovation, teamwork, synergy, growth-mindset, collaboration) more clearly into the prompts themselves, while still keeping the quality high. \n\nThe goal is to get a higher coverage score from the tool so the leadership team sees these as 'aligned' with our culture, but please, keep them professional and don't let them become nonsense. Just a more robust version of the first set.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Here are the reframed behavioral interview questions that incorporate the specific themes of innovation, teamwork, synergy, growth-mindset, and collaboration:\n\n1. How do you measure the impact of innovation on a project's success, and can you give an example of a time when an unconventional approach led to a valuable outcome?\n2. In a team project, you're tasked with refining a process that has been in place for years. What questions do you ask to ensure that you're addressing the root causes of inefficiency, and how do you balance the desire for improvement with the need to maintain existing workflows?\n3. Describe a situation where you had to navigate conflicting opinions and priorities within a team to drive progress towards a common goal. How did you foster an environment of constructive debate, and what role did you play in finding a solution that met everyone's needs?\n4. Tell me about a time when you were part of a team that introduced a new tool or process. How did you contribute to its development and implementation, and what successes did you achieve in terms of adoption and usage?\n5. Reframe a recent failure or setback as an opportunity for growth and learning. What factors contributes to your ability to view failures as opportunities for improvement, and can you",
      "source": "target",
